  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The state of Georgia attracted $8B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 72,643 awards with an average award size of $110K, and obligations fell 31.6% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in Georgia, obligating $4.3B so far this fiscal year, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services with $1.2B and the General Services Administration with $981M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry by obligations at $1B, followed by Aircraft Manufacturing at $713M and Engineering Services at $638M. FY2026 to date obligations of $8B compare with FY25 obligations of $11.6B and with prior years that ranged from $8.5B to $11.6B. Current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date, and represent contract obligations rather than completed payments or contract awards closed for the full year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove the year-over-year change in obligations?",
        "body": "The overall obligations fell 31.6% year over year, with total FY2026 to date obligations at $8B compared with FY25 obligations of $11.6B. The Department of Defense remains the single largest buyer, obligating $4.3B, which is a dominant share of the current-year total. Several large industry categories continue to receive funding, but the state-level total is lower than the most recent full fiscal year."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest buyers and vendors in Georgia right now?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency with $4.3B in obligations, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services with $1.2B and the General Services Administration with $981M. LOCKHEED MARTIN CORP is the largest vendor with $647M in obligations, followed by MANHATTAN TORCON A JOINT VENTURE with $492M and GEORGIA TECH APPLIED RESEARCH CORP with $256M."
      }
    ]
  },
